        "titel": "The foundational three: Marx, Durkheim and Weber",
        "tekst": "*(Box.)* Since the 1950s, **Marx, Durkheim and Weber** have been taught as the 'holy trinity' of sociology's founders: each gives a core understanding of modern capitalist society, the Industrial Revolution and the political transformations of the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries.\n\n**Marx (1818-83)**: 'the history of all hitherto existing societies is the history of **class struggle**' (*The Communist Manifesto*). People are born into a history not of their making, but they also make history. Industrial capitalism brings **exploitation** and a suffering lower class; as people become aware of their situation, **revolution** would create a new order of equality. 'The ideas of the ruling class are in every epoch the ruling ideas.' He inspired communist societies (more than a fifth of the world in the mid-twentieth century, now seen as authoritarian failures), and his work keeps oppression, conflict, inequality and exploitation central in sociology.\n\n**Durkheim (1858-1917)**: societies move from great similarity to a rapid growth of the **division of labour**: from **mechanical to organic** society, which can bring a breakdown of integration and **anomie**, a state of normlessness; new groups (guilds) could restore community. An architect of the structural-functional tradition; sociology should **'treat social facts as things'**. The only one of the three to work in a sociology department.\n\n**Weber (1864-1920)**: societies become increasingly dominated by **rational thought** and **bureaucracy**, bringing benefits but also **'disenchantment'** and an **'iron cage'**; religions would decline; capitalism arose mainly through the individualistic **Protestant ethic**. He focused on human actions and meanings, struggled with sociology as **value-free**, and was the most pessimistic of the three. *(The book's 'Marx (1803-83)' at the top of the box is a misprint; elsewhere it gives 1818-83.)*"
